.TH LIBKECCAK_K12 7 LIBKECCAK
.SH NAME
libkeccak_k12 - streaming byte-oriented KT128 (KangarooTwelve) XOF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <libkeccak/k12.h>

struct libkeccak_k12 *libkeccak_k12_create(void);
int libkeccak_k12_update(struct libkeccak_k12 *\fIctx\fP, const void *\fIdata\fP, size_t \fIlength\fP);
int libkeccak_k12_final(struct libkeccak_k12 *\fIctx\fP, const void *\fIcustom\fP, size_t \fIlength\fP);
int libkeccak_k12_squeeze(struct libkeccak_k12 *\fIctx\fP, void *\fIout\fP, size_t \fIlength\fP);
void libkeccak_k12_destroy(struct libkeccak_k12 *\fIctx\fP);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR libkeccak_k12
implements KT128, the 128-bit-security KangarooTwelve extendable-output
function of RFC 9861, as a byte-oriented streaming interface for
fingerprinting inputs that are too large to assemble before hashing.
It uses the TurboSHAKE128 rate of 168 bytes and the Keccak-p[1600,12]
permutation, i.e. the last twelve rounds of Keccak-f[1600]. The existing
SHA-3/SHAKE interfaces keep their full 24-round behaviour.
.PP
The input is the logical string
.IR "S = M || C || E(length(C))" ,
where M is the message, C the customisation string and E the
length encoding. The tree-vs-single-piece decision is made over the whole
of S, not over the message alone: an empty customisation string still
contributes its one-byte encoding E(0). If S fits in one 8192-byte piece
the output is TurboSHAKE128 with domain byte 0x07; otherwise each later
piece is hashed to a 32-byte chaining value with domain byte 0x0b and the
assembled root is hashed with domain byte 0x06.
.SH CALL SEQUENCE
The functions are used in this order:
.IP 1. 4
.BR libkeccak_k12_create ()
allocates a context in the input-accepting phase.
.IP 2. 4
.BR libkeccak_k12_update ()
is called zero or more times, in order, with message chunks. Chunk
boundaries do not affect the result; zero-length updates are allowed and
NUL bytes are ordinary data.
.IP 3. 4
.BR libkeccak_k12_final ()
is called exactly once with the customisation string; it consumes that
string and its length encoding and ends the input. It may fail, in which
case the context stays in the input-accepting phase and final may be
retried. On success update and further final calls fail.
.IP 4. 4
.BR libkeccak_k12_squeeze ()
is then called any number of times. The reads concatenate into one XOF
stream: reading 1, 167, 1, 168 and then the remainder yields the same
bytes as reading the total at once. A zero-length read succeeds and
advances nothing. Squeeze before final fails and does not write the
output buffer.
.IP 5. 4
.BR libkeccak_k12_destroy ()
releases the context; destroying NULL is safe.
.PP
Calls in an illegal phase return -1 without changing the result of later
legal calls. Caller buffers are only borrowed for the duration of a call
and may be reused or overwritten afterwards. Independent contexts share
no mutable state and may be interleaved.
.SH SPACE
The whole context, including every working buffer, occupies a single
fixed-size allocation of about 9 KiB and never exceeds 32 KiB, regardless
of input length. The message, the customisation string and the piece
chaining values are never retained in full: each chaining value is folded
into the root sponge as soon as its 8192-byte piece is complete. No
update, final or squeeze call allocates memory that depends on the input
length.
